    "created_at": "2022-03-11T08:40:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-03-11T08:40:22Z",
    "body": "> Firefox seems to assume the optimum region is exclusive, and other browsers assume the optimum region is inclusive.\r\n\r\nThis isn’t entirely correct. Firefox always assume its exclusive. However, Safari and Chrome assumes the boundary between the low and medium regions are exclusive, and the medium and high regions are inclusive. As shown in this table:\r\n\r\n![Browser compatibility table](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1102886/157828480-159c8c73-9da9-46a2-86b5-45f6affe0251.png)\r\n\r\nHowever, the spec as it’s currently written suggest the low and high attributes should be inclusive. (An attribute named low defining a region called low should logically be included in said region.) So, **no browser is correct.** I’ve just written [an article arguing for this interpretation](https://www.ctrl.blog/entry/html-meter-segment-boundaries.html).",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
